"""The table's codes for these instances are not at a local optimum of the largest cosine. Local search from the
published coordinates lowered the cosine of 46 of the 48 by more than 1e-12 and of 34 by more than 1e-6, by up to
8.1e-04 (10_214); 46 of them beat the table's displayed (rounded-up) cosine by at least 1e-12. The search per
instance, about eight minutes: sequential linear programming on the near-active pairs (sph.maximize), L-BFGS on the
smooth maximum of the cosines at sharpness rising to 1e9 (sph.descend), and two passes of basin hopping
(sph.improve: shake all points by 1e-4 to 1e-2 of the minimal chord, descend, maximize, keep drops). Where a further
minute of descend and maximize still lowered the cosine by more than 1e-7, or a system sleep cut the run short and
it was rerun (24 of the 48), one-minute passes followed while they gained more than 1e-8, up to four, up to twelve
more on the 4 still moving after those and up to twenty more on the 1 still moving after those; the longest instance
took 29 minutes. It does not converge: a further minute of descend and maximize still lowers the cosine by up to
2e-07 on 30 of the 48. Instances where nothing beat the published code keep it. These codes are the bar the task
scores against. COS2 is the exact signed squared cosine of the binary64 points as numerator/denominator, COORDS the
points as base64 of little-endian binary64 rows. SCiEL run spb11 (Qwen3.8-27B) then lowered 42 more, which replace
those. SCiEL run spd11 (Qwen3.8-27B) then lowered 35 more, which replace those."""

